 "method": "contact sheet, packages/transcription/work/broadway-sheet.png",
 "note": "Finale's handwritten face, and the first family here whose text variants outnumber its musical shapes: most of the sheet is the alphabet, because the sticking letters under every note are set in it. Naming them matters as much as naming the notes -- an unnamed glyph on the staff makes its bar suspect, so an unlabelled alphabet would flag the whole repertoire.\nThe three shapes that carry rhythm were read off a cropped page (stainless-snare) rather than the sheet, because the sheet cannot show where a mark sits: the thick slash seen 541 times crosses a stem and is a diddle, and the z beside it is a buzz. Named from the sheet alone, either could have passed for a notehead.\nEight shapes are left unnamed. All are seen fewer than twenty times, and an unnamed glyph costs a flagged bar, which is recoverable, where a wrong note is not.",
